[# Section 8 — tooling: CLI, editors, LSP, tree-sitter, lint, diff,
   cookbook, diagnostics, build integration, doc-gen, playground,
   profiling. Anchors normative depth in
   tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md, tooling/tree-sitter-cx/,
   scripts/gen_docs/, vcx/cmd/.
   File extension: .cxd (data-only). #]

[section id=tooling n=8 title='Tooling'

  [intro
    """The CX toolchain is one binary (`cx`) and a small fleet of editor
       integrations built on top of it. The binary is the same V codebase
       that backs `libcx` — every binding, every CI gate, and every
       editor talks to the same parser, the same canonicalizer, the same
       evaluator. There is no second implementation to drift against.
       This section is the working developer reference for that surface:
       what each subcommand does, how the editor tooling wires up, what
       the linter checks, what the diff reports, and how you embed CX
       into existing build systems."""]

  [child id=cli n=8.1 title='The cx CLI'
    [intro
      """`cx` is the one-stop command-line interface. It evaluates,
         formats, canonicalizes, hashes, compares, diffs, lints,
         validates, converts, and renders CX documents. Every
         subcommand is a thin shim over the same V core
         (`vcx/cmd/main.v` dispatches on `argv[1]`), so behaviour is
         identical to what every host-language binding sees."""]

    [body
      """The CLI follows a small handful of conventions. **Running a
         file is the default action**: `cx program.cx` parses and
         evaluates it (a pure-data document evaluates to itself).
         Input comes from a file path or stdin; output goes to
         stdout. Exit code 0 means success, 1 means a user-visible
         diagnostic (lint finding, diff delta, validation failure,
         non-equivalent compare), 2 means an internal or I/O error.
         Subcommands that gate on severity (`cx lint`, `cx
         validate`) accept `--fail-on=THRESHOLD` to tighten the
         gate; default is `--fail-on=error`."""]

    [body
      """The CLI surface is exercised by the conformance corpora
         under `conformance/*.cxd` (lint, diff, schema-validate,
         delimited projections, …) run by `make test-vcx-suite` —
         the fixtures are the normative reference where this guide
         and the binary disagree."""]

    [child id=cli-overview n=8.1.1 title='Subcommand catalog'
      [body
        """The full shipped set. `cx --help` lists every subcommand —
           the catalog and the dispatch are generated from one
           table in `vcx/cmd/main.v`, so they cannot drift — and
           every subcommand answers `cx <subcommand> --help` with
           its own usage:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'command'] [th 'purpose']]
        [tr [td 'cx FILE'] [td 'Parse + evaluate a document / program (the default action)']]
        [tr [td 'cx fmt FILE'] [td 'Lossless canonical formatter (preserves comments, anchors; normalizes whitespace/quoting)']]
        [tr [td 'cx canonical FILE'] [td 'Strict canonical text (strips presentation; data-equivalent output)']]
        [tr [td 'cx hash FILE'] [td 'SHA-256 hex of the strict-canonical bytes']]
        [tr [td 'cx eq A B'] [td 'Exit 0 iff strict-canonical(A) == strict-canonical(B)']]
        [tr [td 'cx diff A B'] [td 'Semantic diff over strict-canonical forms']]
        [tr [td 'cx lint FILE'] [td 'Style + correctness warnings']]
        [tr [td 'cx validate FILE --schema=S.cxs'] [td 'Schema validation']]
        [tr [td 'cx table VERB'] [td 'Table API surface (info / dump / load)']]
        [tr [td 'cx demo'] [td 'Self-contained showcase, no I/O, < 1s']]
        [tr [td 'cx scaffold KIND'] [td 'Typed, commented skeleton on stdout (config / data / doc / log / table)']]
        [tr [td 'cx lock'] [td 'Generate / verify cx.lock from [?lib] directives']]
        [tr [td 'cx lsp'] [td 'Language server on stdio']]
        [tr [td 'cx diagram FILE'] [td 'Program/data containment diagram (mermaid / svg / png)']]
        [tr [td 'cx code-diagram FILE'] [td 'Sequence diagram of a CX program']]
        [tr [td 'cx code-tree FILE'] [td 'Evaluation-tree rendering of a CX program']]
        [tr [td 'cx store-serve'] [td 'Serve a cx-store (reference store server)']]
        [tr [td 'cx store-health'] [td 'Store health probe']]
        [tr [td 'cx store-token'] [td 'Mint store access tokens']]
        [tr [td 'cx store-rotate-kek'] [td 'Rotate the store key-encryption key']]
        [tr [td 'cx eval FILE'] [td 'Alias of the default run action; prefer cx FILE']]
        [tr [td 'cx select PATH FILE'] [td 'CXPath query over a document — matches in canonical CX, one per line']]
        [tr [td 'cx version'] [td 'Version / build info (same output as -v / --version)']]]

      [body
        """There is **no** `cx parse` / `cx convert` / `cx modify` /
           `cx schema` / `cx help` subcommand — parsing and
           conversion ride the projection flags below, updates are
           programs you run with `cx FILE`, and schema checking is
           `cx validate`. Ad-hoc selection has its own verb:
           `cx select 'PATH' [FILE]` evaluates one CXPath expression
           against a document (see below)."""]]

    [child id=cli-projections n=8.1.2 title='Projection and conversion flags'
      [body
        """Format projection is a top-level flag surface, not a
           subcommand. One flag picks the output projection of an
           input document; `--from` / `--to` drive explicit
           conversions:"""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx --ast config.cx                 # JSON AST
           $ cx --json config.cx                # JSON projection
           $ cx --yaml config.cx                # YAML projection
           $ cx --xml --compact config.cx       # XML, minimised
           $ cx --from=md --to=cx README.md     # Markdown in, CX out
           $ cx --from=cx --to=json --lossless order.cx
           $ echo '[hello world]' | cx --json -  # stdin"""]
      [list
        [item """Projection flags: `--ast` `--cx` `--xml` `--json`
                `--yaml` `--toml` `--md` `--csv` `--tsv` `--psv`,
                each optionally with `--compact`."""]
        [item """`--from=cx|xml|json|yaml|toml|md|csv|tsv|psv
                --to=cx|xml|json|yaml|toml|md|csv|tsv|psv`
                — explicit conversion; `--lossless` makes XML carry
                per-value types (`<cx:T>`) for an exact round-trip."""]
        [item """`--include-root=DIR` — resolve `[?cx include=…]`
                against DIR before projecting (include resolution is
                opt-in; without a root the directive is preserved)."""]]]

    [child id=cli-run-caps n=8.1.3 title='Running programs and capability flags'
      [body
        """`cx program.cx` evaluates a program. A separate data
           input rides `--data=INPUT.cx` (or `--data=-` for stdin):
           the input is loaded via the data reading and bound as
           `$doc` / `$input` before evaluation, and the caller-supplied
           input **wins** — the program's own data roots never rebind
           it. Without `--data`, the program's first data root binds
           implicitly; a document-driven program run without any input
           raises the loud unbound-`$doc` error rather than silently
           matching nothing. The four `examples/` tours run exactly
           this way (`cx examples/code-tour.cx
           --data=examples/code-tour.input.cx`)."""]
      [body
        """Unknown flags on this surface are **hard usage errors**
           (exit 2, naming the flag) — including misspelled
           `--allow-*` grants. Nothing on the run surface is silently
           ignored."""]
      [body
        """Effects are
           **deny-by-default**: a program that reads files, writes
           files, touches the network, reads environment variables,
           spawns subprocesses, or asks for randomness must be
           granted that capability explicitly on the command line
           (denied effect points raise the catchable capability
           error `CXER0271`):"""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx report.cx --data=orders.cx       # orders.cx binds as $doc
           $ cx build_site.cx --allow-read --allow-write
           $ cx serve.cx --allow-net
           $ cx pipeline.cx --allow-all          # every capability"""]
      [body
        """The capability set: `--allow-read`, `--allow-write`,
           `--allow-net`, `--allow-env`, `--allow-clock`,
           `--allow-random`, `--allow-subprocess`, `--allow-eval`,
           `--allow-secret-reveal`, and the blanket `--allow-all`.
           See [[security]] for the security model."""]
      [body
        """A `cx eval FILE` alias exists for the same action; prefer
           the plain `cx FILE` spelling — the alias adds nothing."""]]

    [child id=cli-fmt n=8.1.4 title='cx fmt'
      [body
        """Pretty-prints in **lossless canonical** form. Comments,
           anchors, and authorial structure are preserved;
           whitespace and quoting are normalised. Reads the file
           argument (or stdin) and writes the formatted document to
           stdout."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx fmt config.cx                    # formatted, to stdout
           $ cx fmt config.cx > config.fmt.cx && mv config.fmt.cx config.cx"""]]

    [child id=cli-canon n=8.1.5 title='cx canonical / cx hash / cx eq'
      [body
        """`cx canonical` emits the **strict canonical text** per
           [`spec/canonical.md`](../../spec/canonical.md): comments
           stripped, attributes sorted, anchors expanded, whitespace
           collapsed, quoting normalised. Two data-equivalent inputs
           produce identical output — that is the contract `cx hash`
           (SHA-256 of those bytes) and `cx eq` (exit 0 iff the
           strict-canonical forms match, 1 if they differ, 2 on
           error) build on."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx canonical config.cx | shasum -a 256   # equivalent to cx hash
           $ cx hash config.cx
           $ cx eq prod.cx proposed.cx && echo unchanged || echo changed"""]]

    [child id=cli-diff n=8.1.6 title='cx diff'
      [body
        """Semantic diff — walks the strict-canonical forms, so
           presentation-only edits produce no delta. See [[diff]]
           for the output contract. Flags: `--format=unified|json|summary`
           (default `unified`), `--no-color`. Exit codes match
           `diff(1)`: 0 equal, 1 differs, 2 error."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx diff prod.cx proposed.cx
           $ cx diff --format=json prod.cx proposed.cx
           $ cx diff prod.cx proposed.cx && echo no-op   # CI gate"""]]

    [child id=cli-lint n=8.1.7 title='cx lint'
      [body
        """Style + correctness warnings. See [[lint]] for the rule
           catalog. Flags: `--format=text|json|summary` (default
           `text`), `--fail-on=info|warn|error|none` (default
           `error`), `--disable=ID1,ID2` to suppress checks,
           `--only=ID` to run one."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx lint config.cx
           $ cx lint --fail-on=warn --format=json config.cx
           $ cx lint --only=CX-L004 pipeline.cx"""]]

    [child id=cli-validate n=8.1.8 title='cx validate'
      [body
        """Validates a document against a `.cxs` schema
           ([`spec/schema.md`](../../spec/schema.md)). Flags:
           `--schema=SCHEMA.cxs` (required),
           `--fail-on=info|warn|error|none` (default `error`),
           `--mode=open|strict|closed` (overrides the schema-mode
           directive), `--apply-defaults` (materialise schema-default
           attribute values). Exit 0 if no diagnostics at/above the
           threshold, 1 if any, 2 on I/O or schema failure."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx validate users.cx --schema=users.cxs
           $ cx validate users.cx --schema=users.cxs --mode=strict --fail-on=warn"""]]

    [child id=cli-table n=8.1.9 title='cx table'
      [body
        """The public Table API surface for CXCol / `[table[…]]`
           payloads: `cx table info FILE` (column/row counts, types,
           byte size), `cx table dump FILE --to=cx` (round-trip via
           the Table API), `cx table load FILE --to=cx` (symmetric
           inverse). Parquet / Arrow output (`--to=parquet|arrow`)
           defers to the `libcx_arrow` bridge. See [[analytics]]."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx table info data.cxcol
           $ cx table dump data.cxcol --to=cx | head"""]]

    [child id=cli-diagram n=8.1.10 title='cx diagram / code-diagram / code-tree'
      [body
        """Visualization commands. `cx diagram FILE`
           renders a containment diagram
           (`--format=mermaid|svg|png`, `-o out.file`); `cx
           code-diagram FILE` renders a locked sequence-diagram view
           of a program; `cx code-tree FILE` renders the evaluation
           tree. The playground's Diagram toggle uses the same
           renderers via WASM (see [[playground]])."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx diagram config.cx --format=mermaid
           $ cx code-diagram pipeline.cx
           $ cx code-tree pipeline.cx"""]]

    [child id=cli-scaffold n=8.1.11 title='cx scaffold / cx demo / cx lock'
      [body
        """`cx scaffold KIND` drops a typed, commented skeleton on
           stdout — KIND ∈ `config`, `data`, `doc`, `log`, `table`.
           `cx demo` runs a self-contained showcase (typed
           round-trip, `[table[…]]` → CSV, a CX program) with no
           file I/O and no network. `cx lock` generates or
           verifies `cx.lock` from a project's `[?lib]` directives —
           `--check`, `--update NAME`, `--output PATH`."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx scaffold config > app.cx
           $ cx demo
           $ cx lock --check"""]]

    [child id=cli-lsp n=8.1.12 title='cx lsp'
      [body
        """Starts the language server on stdio (JSON-RPC 2.0 per
           LSP 3.17). See [[lsp]] for the
           capability surface. Editors spawn it on demand — see
           [[editors]]."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx lsp    # editor connects on stdio"""]]

    [child id=cli-select n=8.1.13 title='cx select'
      [body
        """CXPath query over one document —
           `cx select 'PATH' [FILE]` (stdin when FILE is `-` or
           absent). The document binds as `$doc`; PATH is a single
           CXPath value expression (`$doc/…`, `/…`, or `//…`,
           predicates included). Matches print one per line in
           canonical CX, in document order; attribute-axis matches
           materialize as `[name value]` fields. Exit 0 with at
           least one match, 1 on an empty match set (grep-style),
           2 on error. A pure read — no capability grants accepted
           or needed. Contract:
           [`spec/misc/cli.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/cli.md) §3.8."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx select '//user[= $_@role admin]' users.cx
           [user name=Alice role=admin]
           $ cx select '$doc/user@name' users.cx
           [name 'Alice']
           [name 'Bob']
           $ cat users.cx | cx select '//user' && echo matched"""]]]

  [child id=editors n=8.2 title='Editor integration'
    [intro
      """CX ships first-class editor support for VS Code, Neovim,
         Helix, and any editor that speaks LSP. Three layers stack on
         top of the `cx` binary: TextMate grammar for display
         contexts, LSP for live editing intelligence, tree-sitter
         for structural / injection duty. See
         [`tooling/install/README.md`](../../tooling/install/README.md)
         for one-command install scripts."""]

    [child id=editor-vscode n=8.2.1 title='VS Code'
      [body
        """The extension lives at `tooling/vscode/`. It bundles:
           TextMate grammar (`syntaxes/cx.tmLanguage.json`), language
           configuration (brackets, comments), snippets
           (`snippets/cx.json`), and an LSP client that spawns
           `cx lsp` on demand. Install via the published `.vsix` or
           `code --install-extension tooling/vscode/cx-language.vsix`."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """# Build and install locally
           cd tooling/vscode
           npm install
           npm run package        # produces cx-language.vsix
           code --install-extension cx-language.vsix

           # Or from the bundled installer
           bash tooling/install/install-vscode.sh"""]

      [body
        """The extension keys off `.cx`, `.cxs`, and `.cxd`
           file extensions. LSP client config (server path, env vars,
           tracing) lives in the VS Code settings panel under "CX
           Language". A sample workspace settings file is at
           [`tooling/lsp/vscode.example.json`](../../tooling/lsp/vscode.example.json)."""]]

    [child id=editor-neovim n=8.2.2 title='Neovim'
      [body
        """Neovim integration uses `nvim-treesitter` for structural
           highlighting + LSP via `nvim-lspconfig` (or the built-in
           LSP client on Neovim 0.11+). A sample config is at
           [`tooling/lsp/neovim.example.lua`](../../tooling/lsp/neovim.example.lua);
           the install script
           [`tooling/install/install-nvim.sh`](../../tooling/install/install-nvim.sh)
           drops the config block into `~/.config/nvim/lua/cx.lua`
           and registers the LSP."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """# One-shot install
           bash tooling/install/install-nvim.sh

           # Manual: copy the example into init.lua
           cat tooling/lsp/neovim.example.lua >> ~/.config/nvim/init.lua"""]

      [note
        """The tree-sitter grammar is installed via
           `:TSInstall cx` once the parser is available on the
           tree-sitter registry. Until then, the install script
           builds the parser locally from `tooling/tree-sitter-cx/`
           and links it into the Neovim parser-search path."""]]

    [child id=editor-helix n=8.2.3 title='Helix'
      [body
        """Helix consumes the tree-sitter grammar directly and
           connects to `cx lsp` for diagnostics, hover, and
           completion. Drop the
           [`tooling/lsp/helix.example.toml`](../../tooling/lsp/helix.example.toml)
           snippet into your `~/.config/helix/languages.toml`."""]

      [example lang=toml
        """# ~/.config/helix/languages.toml
           [[language]]
           name = "cx"
           scope = "source.cx"
           file-types = ["cx", "cxs", "cxd", "cxl"]
           comment-token = "#"
           indent = { tab-width = 2, unit = "  " }
           language-servers = ["cx-lsp"]

           [language-server.cx-lsp]
           command = "cx"
           args = ["lsp"]"""]]

    [child id=editor-intellij n=8.2.4 title='IntelliJ / JetBrains via LSP'
      [body
        """JetBrains IDEs gain CX support through their generic LSP
           plugin: install "LSP4IJ" (or the Ultimate-edition built-in
           LSP support on 2024.3+), and register `cx lsp` as the
           server for `*.cx`, `*.cxs`, `*.cxd`. TextMate
           highlighting comes from the bundled
           `tooling/syntax/cx.tmLanguage.json` via the TextMate
           plugin. The setup is identical for Rider, GoLand,
           PyCharm, RustRover, and IDEA."""]

      [note
        """A dedicated JetBrains plugin (richer than the LSP-only
           path) is planned; tracked in
           [`ROADMAP.md`](../../ROADMAP.md)."""]]

    [child id=editor-sublime n=8.2.5 title='Sublime / Zed / other LSP clients'
      [body
        """Any editor that speaks LSP 3.17 over stdio can drive the
           CX language server. The minimal configuration is: file
           pattern `*.cx`, command `cx lsp`, transport stdio. The
           install scripts under `tooling/install/` cover the
           common cases; for anything else, the contract is just
           LSP."""]]]

  [child id=lsp n=8.3 title='Language server (LSP)'
    [intro
      """The LSP server provides the same intelligence to every
         editor that speaks LSP. Implementation: `vcx/cmd/lsp.v`
         plus `vcx/cmd/lsp_features.v`. Speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 over
         stdio; no separate binary, no port, no daemon. Run-time
         entry point is `cx lsp`."""]

    [child id=lsp-capabilities n=8.3.1 title='Capabilities'
      [body
        """The server advertises the following capabilities in its
           `initialize` response:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'method'] [th 'status'] [th 'notes']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/didOpen / didChange / didClose'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'full document sync']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/publishDiagnostics'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'parse + lint + schema + CXLS001-004']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/hover'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'element, attr, directive, CXPath']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/completion'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'triggers : / @ $']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/semanticTokens/full'] [td 'shipped'] [td '10-token legend']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/formatting'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'lossless fmt']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/definition'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'cross-include and anchor']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/references'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'anchor + alias + merge']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/codeLens'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'diagram preview, eval result']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/codeAction'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'quick-fix for CXLS001-004']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/signatureHelp'] [td 'planned'] [td 'for directive arg slots']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/inlayHint'] [td 'planned'] [td 'inferred types']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/rename'] [td 'planned'] [td 'cross-include rename']]]

      [body
        """Implementation lives in `vcx/cmd/lsp_features.v`. The
           CXLS001-004 diagnostic
           emitters per
           [`tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md`](../../tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md)."""]]

    [child id=lsp-diagnostics n=8.3.2 title='Diagnostic codes'
      [body
        """Diagnostics flow from three sources, each with its own code
           prefix:"""]

      [list
        [item """**CXER\\*** — runtime / parse errors from the V core.
                See [`spec/cx_native_error_codes.md`](../../spec/cx_native_error_codes.md)
                for the full catalog."""]
        [item """**CX-L\\*** — lint findings from `cx_lint` (see
                [[lint]]); same codes the CLI emits."""]
        [item """**CXLS\\*** — LSP-only static-analysis diagnostics
                that don't have a CLI surface. Currently reserved:"""]]

      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'severity'] [th 'trigger']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS001'] [td 'warning'] [td 'unreachable [?match] arm (after [else …] or [case _ …])']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS002'] [td 'hint'] [td '[?match] with no [else …] (silent () fallout)']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS003'] [td 'hint'] [td 'sibling [case …] arms with similar [when …] predicates (consolidation suggestion)']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS004'] [td 'error'] [td '[?modify] [set-attr] / [delete-attr] on attribute-step path (compile-time mirror of CXER0100)']]]

      [body
        """Codes are stable strings; editor configs may key off them
           to surface custom UI (e.g. fold a CXLS003 cluster into a
           single inlay). See
           [`tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md`](../../tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md)
           for the emit contracts."""]]

    [child id=lsp-hover n=8.3.3 title='Hover'
      [body
        """Hover content depends on where the cursor sits:"""]

      [list
        [item """**Element name** — element-doc from the active
                schema (if any), attribute table, link to the schema
                source line."""]
        [item """**Attribute name** — type, default, schema source."""]
        [item """**Directive name** — one-line synopsis + link to the
                directive page in this guide."""]
        [item """**CXPath path expression** — resolved XPath
                production, live match-count against the current
                document, axis-name doc."""]
        [item """**Layer-2 host idiom** — desugared Layer-1 form
                (Python comprehension, Go filter chain, Rust
                iterator). Implementation calls
                `cxlib.idioms.explain()`."""]]

      [example lang=cx
        """[# Hovering '//section[2]/p[@lang=\"en\"]' shows: #]
           [# /section[2]/p[@lang=\"en\"]                    #]
           [# → child::section[position()=2]/child::p[@lang=\"en\"] #]
           [# → matches 3 nodes in this document             #]
           [# → axis `child::` selects direct children (default) #]"""]]

    [child id=lsp-completion n=8.3.4 title='Completion'
      [body
        """Completion triggers on `:`, `@`, `$`, and `/`. The trigger
           character determines the candidate set:"""]

      [list
        [item """**`:`** — type tags (`:int`, `:string`, `:date`...),
                directive names (`[?for`, `[?match`...), labeled-slot
                names inside an active directive."""]
        [item """**`@`** — attribute names from the schema or the
                live document."""]
        [item """**`$`** — variable names from `[?let]` bindings in
                scope."""]
        [item """**`/`** — CXPath axes + element names + node-kind
                tests."""]]

      [body
        """All completion items carry `data.kind` so editors can
           sort and group them. CXPath items are tagged
           `data.path_axis=true` per
           [`tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md`](../../tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md)."""]]

    [child id=lsp-codeactions n=8.3.5 title='Code actions'
      [body
        """Code actions are quick-fixes the editor offers for
           diagnostics. Currently shipped:"""]

      [list
        [item """**CXLS001** (unreachable arm) — "remove unreachable
                arm". Deletes the arm."""]
        [item """**CXLS002** (no `[else …]`) — "add `[else]` arm".
                Inserts an empty `[else ()]` clause."""]
        [item """**CX-L001** (comment style) — "convert `[# #]` to
                `# line comment`" (and vice versa)."""]
        [item """**CX-L002** (type-alias style) — "expand to
                long-form" / "shorten to alias"."""]
        [item """**CX-L003** (unused anchor) — "remove anchor"."""]
        [item """**CX-L004** (dangling alias) — "create missing
                anchor" (with a placeholder body)."""]]

      [body
        """CXLS003 consolidation auto-fix is planned
           (recognise-only currently per
           [`tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md`](../../tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md)
           §out-of-scope)."""]]

    [child id=lsp-semantic-tokens n=8.3.6 title='Semantic tokens'
      [body
        """The LSP semanticTokens response is the **canonical
           highlighting source** for editors that consume it.
           The 10-token legend maps directly to TextMate scopes for
           cross-editor consistency:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'token'] [th 'example']]
        [tr [td 'namespace'] [td 'module prefixes, schema-qualified names']]
        [tr [td 'keyword'] [td 'directive names ([?for], [?if], [?match]...)']]
        [tr [td 'variable'] [td '$ctx, $-bound names from [?let]']]
        [tr [td 'parameter'] [td 'function-parameter names and pattern $bindings']]
        [tr [td 'property'] [td 'attribute names']]
        [tr [td 'string'] [td 'string literals (single, double, triple quoted)']]
        [tr [td 'number'] [td 'int, float, hex, sized-type literals']]
        [tr [td 'comment'] [td '[# block #] and # line comments']]
        [tr [td 'operator'] [td '= + - * / sigils, CXPath operators']]
        [tr [td 'decorator'] [td 'type annotations (:int, :date, ...)']]]]

    [child id=lsp-debugging n=8.3.7 title='Debugging the server'
      [body
        """Run with `--verbose` and pipe stderr to a file to trace
           incoming methods. The server logs each request, its
           dispatch path, and timing."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx lsp --verbose 2>/tmp/cx-lsp.log
           $ tail -f /tmp/cx-lsp.log
           [lsp] initialize ← capabilities: hoverProvider=true, ...
           [lsp] textDocument/didOpen ← config.cx (1342 bytes)
           [lsp] textDocument/hover @ 12:8 → 'host' element-doc"""]

      [body
        """Smoke-test the JSON-RPC handshake without an editor:"""]

      [example lang=sh
        """python3 - <<'EOF' | cx lsp
           import sys, json
           def msg(o):
               b = json.dumps(o)
               sys.stdout.write(f\"Content-Length: {len(b)}\\r\\n\\r\\n{b}\")
           msg({\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{}})
           msg({\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"shutdown\"})
           msg({\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"exit\"})
           EOF"""]]]

  [child id=tree-sitter n=8.4 title='Tree-sitter grammar'
    [intro
      """`tooling/tree-sitter-cx/` is the tree-sitter grammar for CX.
         Tree-sitter is scoped to **structural and embedded-language-
         injection duty** — element / attribute / scalar tokenisation
         + opaque-PI rendering of `[? ... ]` directives. Per-directive
         highlighting comes from the LSP semanticTokens, not from
         tree-sitter."""]

    [child id=ts-shape n=8.4.1 title='Grammar shape'
      [body
        """The grammar (`grammar.js`) covers everything in
           [`spec/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/grammar.ebnf) at the
           **structural** level: bracketed elements, attribute key=value
           pairs, scalar literals (string / int / float / bool / date /
           datetime / null), sigil-prefixed attributes (`#id`,
           `&anchor`, `*alias`), collection literals
           (sequence `(...)`, map `{...}`, array — see
           [[code#collection-literals]]), and comments (`[# #]` and
           `# line`). Directives — every `[?Name ...]` form — are
           tokenised as a single opaque `(directive)` region; the
           internal labeled slots and bodies are not pre-parsed at
           the tree-sitter layer."""]]

    [child id=ts-queries n=8.4.2 title='Highlight + injection queries'
      [body
        """Queries live in `tooling/tree-sitter-cx/queries/`:"""]

      [list
        [item """**`highlights.scm`** — structural scopes only
                (element-name, attribute-name, string, number,
                comment, directive-opaque). Per-directive interior
                highlighting is delegated to LSP semanticTokens."""]
        [item """**`injections.scm`** — embedded-language injection
                for fenced code blocks (`[code lang='rust'
                [| ... |]]`). Recognised injection languages:
                `python`, `rust`, `go`, `js`, `ts`, `java`, `c`,
                `cpp`, `csharp`, `ruby`, `swift`, `kotlin`, `sh`,
                `sql`, `html`, `css`, `json`, `yaml`, `toml`,
                `xml`, `markdown`."""]
        [item """**`indents.scm`** — auto-indent rules. CX is
                bracket-structured; indent inside `[`, dedent at
                `]`."""]
        [item """**`locals.scm`** — variable scoping for `[?let]`
                bindings."""]]]

    [child id=ts-regen n=8.4.3 title='Regenerating the parser'
      [body
        """The committed `src/parser.c` is generated from
           `grammar.js` by `tree-sitter generate`. The `make
           tree-sitter` target rebuilds it; CI gates that the
           committed `parser.c` matches `grammar.js`."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cd tooling/tree-sitter-cx
           $ npm install
           $ npx tree-sitter generate         # regenerate src/parser.c
           $ npx tree-sitter test             # run grammar tests
           $ npx tree-sitter parse sample.cx  # smoke-test on a file"""]

      [note
        """`make tree-sitter` from the repo root wraps all of the
           above and is what the CI lane runs."""]]]

  [child id=lint n=8.5 title='Lint rules'
    [intro
      """`cx lint` warns about issues a formatter can't or shouldn't
         fix automatically. Lint and fmt are
         distinct: `cx fmt` *applies* safe transformations; `cx lint`
         *warns* about issues that need human judgement to fix. The
         two never overlap — if a check has a safe auto-fix, it
         belongs to `cx fmt`."""]

    [child id=lint-catalog n=8.5.1 title='Rule catalog'
      [body
        """Five lint checks are currently shipped:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'id'] [th 'severity'] [th 'name']]
        [tr [td 'CX-L001'] [td 'info'] [td 'comment-style consistency']]
        [tr [td 'CX-L002'] [td 'info'] [td 'type-annotation form consistency']]
        [tr [td 'CX-L003'] [td 'warn'] [td 'unused anchor']]
        [tr [td 'CX-L004'] [td 'error'] [td 'dangling alias / merge']]
        [tr [td 'CX-L005'] [td 'warn'] [td 'deprecated v3.3→v3.4 pattern']]]

      [body
        """Each check has a normative ID, a default severity, and a
           documented rationale. Suppression directives and CLI flags accept the ID
           verbatim."""]]

    [child id=lint-severity n=8.5.2 title='Severity levels'
      [body
        """Three severity levels, normative names:"""]

      [list
        [item """**`info`** — stylistic drift. Doesn't affect
                correctness. Doesn't fail CI by default."""]
        [item """**`warn`** — likely-bug. The code parses, but the
                result probably isn't what was intended. Fails CI
                with `--fail-on=warn`."""]
        [item """**`error`** — almost-certainly-bug. Document
                semantics are wrong. Fails CI by default
                (`--fail-on=error`)."""]]]

    [child id=lint-config n=8.5.3 title='Configuration via .cxlint.cx'
      [body
        """Per-project lint config lives in `.cxlint.cx` at the repo
           root (or any ancestor directory of the linted file). The
           config file is CX-formatted — same parser, same schema,
           dogfooded all the way down."""]

      [example lang=cx
        """[# .cxlint.cx — repo-level lint config #]
           [lint
             [# ignore unused anchors in the legacy templates dir #]
             [disable check=CX-L003 path='legacy/**']

             [# v3.3 transition warnings only as info, we'll fix gradually #]
             [severity check=CX-L005 level=info]

             [# treat dangling-alias as warn (not error) in this repo #]
             [severity check=CX-L004 level=warn]]"""]

      [body
        """Path globs follow `.gitignore` syntax. `cx lint --config
           PATH` overrides auto-discovery. Missing config file is
           not an error — defaults apply."""]]

    [child id=lint-suppress n=8.5.4 title='Per-element suppression'
      [body
        """For one-off suppressions inside a document, use the
           `[?cx lint-disable=...]` / `[?cx lint-enable=...]`
           directives. Scope is the enclosing element and its
           descendants."""]

      [example lang=cx
        """[?cx lint-disable=CX-L003]
           [defaults &base host=localhost]   [# no L003 warning #]
           [?cx lint-enable=CX-L003]

           [# lint-disable=all suppresses every check #]
           [?cx lint-disable=all]
           [legacy-block ...]"""]

      [note
        """Comma-separated lists accepted:
           `lint-disable=CX-L003,CX-L005`. `lint-disable=all` is the
           catch-all."""]]

    [child id=lint-cxls n=8.5.5 title='LSP-only diagnostic codes (CXLS\\*)'
      [body
        """The CXLS\\* codes ([[lsp-diagnostics]]) are surfaced only
           through the LSP and have no corresponding `cx lint`
           check. They live in the LSP because they need
           document-wide static analysis (cross-arm reachability,
           focus-path type-check) and aren't general-enough to be
           CLI default behaviour. Suppression inside a document
           uses `[?cx lint-disable=CXLS001]` (same syntax as the
           CX-L\\* codes); CLI suppression is moot since `cx lint`
           doesn't emit them."""]]

    [child id=lint-custom n=8.5.6 title='Custom rule plugins (planned)'
      [body
        """A plugin model for user-defined checks is planned
           (previously rejected; the question is reopened in
           [`ROADMAP.md`](../../ROADMAP.md) "Later"). Currently,
           extending the check set means contributing a check to
           `vcx/cx/lint.v` upstream. Schema-violation checks are a
           separate path — they layer in automatically as the schema
           language gains rules."""]]]

  [child id=diff n=8.6 title='Diff semantics'
    [intro
      """`cx diff` produces a **semantic** diff — tree-structured,
         canonical-byte-aware, formatting-blind. Two
         data-equivalent inputs diff to nothing, even if they differ
         at the text level. This is what makes `cx diff` usable as a
         CI gate: it reports semantic deltas, not formatting noise."""]

    [child id=diff-scope n=8.6.1 title='What cx diff reports (and ignores)'
      [body
        """`cx diff` reports differences in the strict canonical form
           ([`spec/canonical.md §1.2`](../../spec/canonical.md)).
           Specifically it **reports**:"""]

      [list
        [item """Element added / removed (by canonical path)."""]
        [item """Element renamed (different canonical name at the
                same path)."""]
        [item """Attribute added / removed / value-changed (by
                canonical name)."""]
        [item """Body content changed (atomic-value or text-content
                differences)."""]
        [item """Type annotation changed (e.g., `:int` vs `:u32` —
                the annotation IS part of the data)."""]
        [item """Element order changed *when order is
                data-significant* (per
                [`spec/canonical.md §2.1`](../../spec/canonical.md))."""]]

      [body
        """And **ignores**:"""]

      [list
        [item """Comments (`# line` and `[# block #]`)."""]
        [item """Attribute order on the same element."""]
        [item """Anchor / alias / merge details — expanded to the
                resolved form before diff."""]
        [item """Whitespace, indentation, line breaks."""]
        [item """Numeric formatting (`1_000_000` vs `1000000`)."""]
        [item """Quoting style (`'foo'` vs `"foo"`)."""]
        [item """Element-name aliases that resolve to the same
                canonical name."""]]]

    [child id=diff-output n=8.6.2 title='Output formats'
      [body
        """Three output modes via `--format`:"""]

      [list
        [item """**`unified`** (default) — element-rooted hunks,
                `-` / `+` / ` ` prefixes. Human-readable."""]
        [item """**`json`** — structured array of change records.
                Suitable for CI gates and editor integrations."""]
        [item """**`summary`** — one-line counts. Useful for shell
                prompts and PR bots."""]]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx diff prod.cx proposed.cx
           --- prod.cx
           +++ proposed.cx
           @@ /config/database @@
            host='primary.db'
           -port=5432
           +port=5433
            replica='secondary.db'"""]

      [example lang=json
        """[
             {
               \"kind\": \"attribute-changed\",
               \"path\": \"/config/database/@port\",
               \"before\": {\"name\": \"port\", \"value\": 5432, \"type\": \"int\"},
               \"after\":  {\"name\": \"port\", \"value\": 5433, \"type\": \"int\"}
             }
           ]"""]]

    [child id=diff-json n=8.6.3 title='JSON change-record shape'
      [body
        """Each record has a stable shape:"""]

      [list
        [item """`kind` — one of `element-added`, `element-removed`,
                `element-renamed`, `attribute-added`,
                `attribute-removed`, `attribute-changed`,
                `body-changed`, `type-changed`, `order-changed`."""]
        [item """`path` — CXPath expression locating the change. The
                same expression drops straight into a CX program
                (`[?for [in $x PATH] …]`)."""]
        [item """`before` / `after` — relevant value for the kind.
                Absent when the side has no value (e.g.
                `element-added` has no `before`). The field is
                **omitted**, not `null` — per
                [`spec/policies.md §2.6`](../../spec/policies.md)'s
                four-way null/empty/missing distinction this is
                *missing*."""]]]

    [child id=diff-git n=8.6.4 title='Git integration'
      [body
        """`cx diff` plugs into `git diff` via the standard custom-
           diff-driver mechanism. Add this to `.gitattributes`:"""]

      [example lang=text
        """*.cx    diff=cx
           *.cxd   diff=cx
           *.cxs   diff=cx
           *.cxcol diff=cx-binary"""]

      [example lang=sh
        """# One-time per repo
           git config diff.cx.command 'cx diff'
           git config diff.cx-binary.command 'cx diff --binary'

           # Or scaffold it
           cx scaffold gitattributes >> .gitattributes"""]

      [body
        """With this in place, `git diff path/to/config.cx` and
           `git show HEAD path/to/config.cx` use the semantic diff
           automatically. PR review UIs that respect `.gitattributes`
           (GitHub, GitLab, Gerrit) pick it up too."""]]

    [child id=diff-exit-codes n=8.6.5 title='Exit codes'
      [body
        """Match `diff(1)`:"""]

      [list
        [item """**`0`** — inputs are data-equivalent (no
                differences)."""]
        [item """**`1`** — inputs differ (at least one semantic
                delta)."""]
        [item """**`2`** — error (parse failure, file not found,
                invalid arguments)."""]]

      [example lang=sh
        """# Use directly as a CI gate
           cx diff prod.cx proposed.cx && echo no-change || review-changes"""]]]

  [child id=cookbook n=8.7 title='Cookbook — common recipes'
    [intro
      """Worked examples of CLI compositions that show up repeatedly
         in real use. Pair-and-match — the CX CLI is designed to
         compose along the standard Unix pipe."""]

    [child id=recipe-convert n=8.7.1 title='Filter JSON, project to YAML'
      [body
        """Read JSON, query / filter / transform via a CX program,
           project the result to YAML."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx --cx input.json > input.cx        # JSON in, CX out
           $ cat input.cx filter.cx | cx > result.cx
           $ cx --yaml result.cx                  # project to YAML"""]

      [body
        """The CX form is the pivot — every conversion is a
           round-trip through the same AST. Appending a program to
           its data (`cat data.cx program.cx | cx`) evaluates the
           program against the preceding document."""]]

    [child id=recipe-validate n=8.7.2 title='Validate config before deploy'
      [body
        """Strict-profile validation, JSON output for the CI
           summary."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx validate config.cx --schema=config.cxs --mode=strict --fail-on=warn \\
             && deploy.sh"""]]

    [child id=recipe-diff n=8.7.3 title='Semantic diff between two YAML files'
      [body
        """Cross-format diff: read YAML on both sides, diff the
           canonical CX form."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx diff a.yaml b.yaml --format summary
           3 attributes changed, 1 element added"""]]

    [child id=recipe-hash n=8.7.4 title='Content hash as cache key'
      [body
        """Use the canonical-bytes hash as a cache key in a build
           pipeline. The hash is stable across formatting changes —
           reformatting `config.cx` will not bust the cache, but
           changing a value will."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ KEY=$(cx hash config.cx)
           $ test -f cache/$KEY.bin || compile config.cx > cache/$KEY.bin
           $ cp cache/$KEY.bin out.bin"""]]

    [child id=recipe-csv-parquet n=8.7.5 title='Convert CSV to Parquet via CX'
      [body
        """Inspect and round-trip a columnar payload through the
           Table API; Parquet / Arrow output rides the
           `libcx_arrow` bridge."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx table info data.cxcol
           $ cx table dump data.cxcol --to=cx | head
           [# --to=parquet|arrow defers to the libcx_arrow bridge (Phase C) #]"""]]

    [child id=recipe-lint-tree n=8.7.6 title='Lint a whole tree before commit'
      [body
        """Recursive lint pre-commit, with shared config."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ find . -name '*.cx' -print0 \\
             | xargs -0 cx lint --format=summary --fail-on=warn"""]]

    [child id=recipe-docs n=8.7.7 title='Render the canonical guide'
      [body
        """Build the canonical guide (this document) from sources.
           See [[docgen]] for the pipeline."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ make docs
           # outputs into _docs_staging/

           $ make docs-diff
           # diff staging vs live publish

           $ make docs-publish
           # copy staging → docs/"""]]

    [child id=recipe-ast-bin n=8.7.8 title='Inspect the AST'
      [body
        """Read the JSON AST — useful when debugging how the parser
           shaped a document."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx --ast doc.cx | jq '.name, (.items | length)'"""]]

    [child id=recipe-roundtrip n=8.7.9 title='Round-trip through every projection'
      [body
        """Assert that a projection round-trip is data-lossless.
           `--lossless` XML carries per-value types, so the
           round-tripped document is strict-canonical-equal to the
           original — the identity contract from
           [[canon-cross-format]] as a CI one-liner."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx --from=cx --to=xml --lossless doc.cx \\
             | cx --from=xml --to=cx - > back.cx
           $ cx eq doc.cx back.cx && echo lossless"""]]]

  [child id=diagnostics n=8.8 title='Diagnostics and debugging'
    [intro
      """When a CX program isn't doing what you expect, work the
         ladder: read the error value (every failure is a
         position-carrying `[err …]`), lint the file, visualize the
         program (`cx code-tree` / `cx code-diagram`), reach for the
         LSP if you want to drive from the editor, and capture a
         fixture (`conformance/code.cxd`) when you need a
         reproducible bug report."""]

    [child id=diag-trace n=8.8.1 title='Errors carry positions'
      [body
        """Every parse or evaluation failure is a structured error
           with a source position and a stable code — read it
           first. A raised error that reaches the top level prints
           as an `[err code=… message=…]` value; the message names
           the offending line:column and, for retired syntax, the
           current replacement form."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx broken.cx
           error: cx-err:CXER0100: parse: infix attribute comparison
           `[@active=…]` in a CXPath predicate is retired — write the
           prefix operator form `[= $_@active …]` (code.md §5.5.2)
           at line 3:20"""]]

    [child id=diag-explain n=8.8.2 title='Visualize the program'
      [body
        """`cx code-tree FILE` renders the evaluation tree of a
           program — which directives nest where, what each binds.
           `cx code-diagram FILE` renders the locked
           sequence-diagram view (concurrency, channels, service
           interactions). `cx --ast FILE` shows the parsed shape
           when you suspect the parser saw something different from
           what you meant."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx code-tree pipeline.cx
           $ cx code-diagram workers.cx
           $ cx --ast pipeline.cx | jq '.items[0]'"""]]

    [child id=diag-lsp n=8.8.3 title='LSP-driven debugging'
      [body
        """The LSP surfaces diagnostics live in the editor —
           parse errors, lint findings, and hover documentation
           over directives and paths. See [[lsp]] for the shipped
           capability set."""]]

    [child id=diag-repl n=8.8.4 title='Interactive REPL'
      [body
        """An interactive REPL is **planned**. Until it ships, the
           equivalent is stdin evaluation with a quick shell
           wrapper — append each expression to the context document
           and pipe through `cx`:"""]

      [example lang=sh
        """# Poor man's REPL
           while read -r line; do
             printf '%s\\n%s\\n' \"$(cat ctx.cx)\" \"$line\" | cx
           done"""]]

    [child id=diag-repro n=8.8.5 title='Reproducing a bug as a conformance fixture'
      [body
        """When you find behaviour that differs from the spec or
           from another binding, the fastest way to land a fix is
           to drop a minimal repro into the conformance corpus.
           Fixtures under `conformance/*.cxd` are the normative
           contract — every binding runs them in CI."""]

      [body
        """Fixtures are CX documents — one `[case …]` element per
           repro, with the input document, the program, and the
           expected output as children:"""]

      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[case id=my-bug-id level=core
             [tags predicate my-area]
             [in-cx [#
           [doc [item id=1] [item id=2]]
           #]]
             [in-code [#
           [?for [in $i //item[= $_@id 1]] [yield $i]]
           #]]
             [out-text [#
           [item id=1]
           #]]]"""]
      [; check=none — fixture-format excerpt: the raw spans hold
         fixture payloads, not a runnable program. ]

      [body
        """Drop the case into the corpus file that matches your
           area (e.g. `conformance/code.cxd` for evaluator bugs,
           `conformance/core.cxd` for data-layer bugs,
           `conformance/lint.cxd` for linter bugs), run
           `make test-vcx-suite`, watch it fail. Then fix the V
           core to make it pass."""]]

    [child id=diag-bug-report n=8.8.6 title='Filing a bug report'
      [body
        """A good bug report has four pieces:"""]

      [list
        [item """`cx --version` output (build + commit line)."""]
        [item """A minimal reproducer — ideally a `conformance/*.cxd`
                case — that triggers the bad behaviour."""]
        [item """Expected output (what you think should happen)."""]
        [item """Actual output (what does happen)."""]]

      [body
        """For LSP / editor bugs, also include the
           `cx lsp --verbose 2>...` log around the failing request
           and the editor's LSP trace setting (most editors expose
           "LSP: Toggle Trace" or similar)."""]]]

  [child id=build-integration n=8.9 title='Build-system integration'
    [intro
      """`cx` is built to slot into the build system you already
         have. The CLI returns conventional exit codes, reads/writes
         on stdio, and produces deterministic output — all the
         properties that let it compose with `make`, `cargo`, `npm`,
         `bazel`, and CI systems without surprises."""]

    [child id=build-make n=8.9.1 title='Make'
      [body
        """A typical pattern: per-`.cxs` schema validation rule,
           gated `cx lint` lane, generated outputs as first-class
           targets."""]

      [example lang=makefile
        """CX        := cx
           CX_FILES  := $(shell find config -name '*.cx')
           CX_STAMPS := $(CX_FILES:.cx=.cx.valid)

           %.cx.valid: %.cx config/config.cxs
           \t$(CX) validate $< --schema=config/config.cxs --mode=strict
           \t@touch $@

           .PHONY: lint
           lint:
           \t$(CX) lint --fail-on=warn $(CX_FILES)

           .PHONY: validate
           validate: $(CX_STAMPS)

           .PHONY: check
           check: lint validate"""]]

    [child id=build-cargo n=8.9.2 title='Cargo (Rust build.rs)'
      [body
        """Use `build.rs` to validate `.cxs`/`.cx` pairs at compile
           time; once the planned schema-codegen tool ships
           the same step generates Rust types directly
           from the schema."""]

      [example lang=rust
        """// build.rs
           use std::process::Command;

           fn main() {
               println!(\"cargo:rerun-if-changed=config/config.cxs\");
               println!(\"cargo:rerun-if-changed=config/default.cx\");

               let status = Command::new(\"cx\")
                   .args([\"validate\", \"config/default.cx\",
                          \"--schema\", \"config/config.cxs\",
                          \"--mode=strict\"])
                   .status()
                   .expect(\"failed to run cx validate — is cx on PATH?\");

               if !status.success() {
                   panic!(\"config validation failed\");
               }

               // Planned: codegen
               // Command::new(\"cx\")
               //     .args([\"schema\", \"codegen\", \"--target\", \"rust\",
               //            \"config/config.cxs\",
               //            \"--output\", \"src/config_gen.rs\"])
               //     .status()
               //     .unwrap();
           }"""]]

    [child id=build-npm n=8.9.3 title='npm scripts'
      [body
        """Wire `cx validate` and `cx lint` into `package.json`
           lifecycle scripts."""]

      [example lang=json
        """{
             \"scripts\": {
               \"lint:cx\":     \"cx lint --fail-on=warn config/**/*.cx\",
               \"validate:cx\": \"cx validate config/app.cx --schema=config/app.cxs --mode=strict\",
               \"pretest\":     \"npm run lint:cx && npm run validate:cx\",
               \"prebuild\":    \"npm run validate:cx\"
             }
           }"""]]

    [child id=build-bazel n=8.9.4 title='Bazel'
      [body
        """A custom rule wrapping `cx validate` for hermetic builds.
           Sketch (treats validation as a generated stamp file):"""]

      [example lang=python
        """# tools/cx.bzl
           def _cx_validate_impl(ctx):
               stamp = ctx.actions.declare_file(ctx.label.name + \".valid\")
               ctx.actions.run_shell(
                   inputs  = [ctx.file.src, ctx.file.schema],
                   outputs = [stamp],
                   command = '''
                       cx validate {src} --schema={schema} --mode=strict \\
                         && touch {out}
                   '''.format(
                       src    = ctx.file.src.path,
                       schema = ctx.file.schema.path,
                       out    = stamp.path,
                   ),
               )
               return [DefaultInfo(files = depset([stamp]))]

           cx_validate = rule(
               implementation = _cx_validate_impl,
               attrs = {
                   \"src\":    attr.label(allow_single_file = [\".cx\", \".cxd\"]),
                   \"schema\": attr.label(allow_single_file = [\".cxs\"]),
               },
           )"""]]

    [child id=build-ci-github n=8.9.5 title='GitHub Actions'
      [body
        """Lint + validate as a PR gate. Reusable workflow that
           installs `cx` from the release artefacts, runs the
           toolchain, fails the PR on diagnostics."""]

      [example lang=yaml
        """# .github/workflows/cx.yml
           name: CX checks
           on: [pull_request]

           jobs:
             cx:
               runs-on: ubuntu-latest
               steps:
                 - uses: actions/checkout@v4
                 - name: Install cx
                   run: |
                     curl -sSfL https://cx-home.dev/install.sh | sh
                     echo \"$HOME/.cx/bin\" >> $GITHUB_PATH
                 - name: cx fmt --check
                   run: find . -name '*.cx' -print0 | xargs -0 cx fmt --check
                 - name: cx lint
                   run: cx lint --fail-on=warn $(find . -name '*.cx')
                 - name: cx validate
                   run: |
                     for f in config/**/*.cx; do
                       cx validate \"$f\" --schema=config/schema.cxs --mode=strict
                     done
                 - name: cx diff (vs base)
                   run: |
                     git fetch origin ${{ github.base_ref }}
                     for f in $(git diff --name-only origin/${{ github.base_ref }} | grep '\\.cx$'); do
                       cx diff <(git show origin/${{ github.base_ref }}:\"$f\") \"$f\" --format summary
                     done"""]

      [body
        """`cx scaffold github-workflow` emits this file as a
           starting point."""]]

    [child id=build-ci-gitlab n=8.9.6 title='GitLab CI'
      [body
        """Equivalent for GitLab CI:"""]

      [example lang=yaml
        """# .gitlab-ci.yml
           cx:
             image: ghcr.io/cx-home/cx:0.8.0
             script:
               - find . -name '*.cx' -print0 | xargs -0 cx fmt --check
               - cx lint --fail-on=warn $(find . -name '*.cx')
               - for f in config/**/*.cx; do cx validate \"$f\" --schema=config/schema.cxs --mode=strict; done
             rules:
               - if: $CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == 'merge_request_event'"""]]

    [child id=build-pre-commit n=8.9.7 title='Pre-commit hooks'
      [body
        """Wire `cx fmt --check` and `cx lint` into the
           [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com/) framework."""]

      [example lang=yaml
        """# .pre-commit-config.yaml
           repos:
             - repo: local
               hooks:
                 - id: cx-fmt
                   name: cx fmt --check
                   entry: cx fmt --check
                   language: system
                   files: '\\.(cx|cxd|cxs|cxl)$'
                 - id: cx-lint
                   name: cx lint
                   entry: cx lint --fail-on=warn
                   language: system
                   files: '\\.(cx|cxd)$'
                 - id: cx-validate
                   name: cx validate (strict)
                   entry: cx validate --schema=config/schema.cxs --mode=strict
                   language: system
                   files: '^config/.*\\.cx$'"""]

      [note
        """`cx scaffold pre-commit` emits the same snippet."""]]]

  [child id=docgen n=8.10 title='Doc-gen pipeline'
    [intro
      """The documentation site (and this canonical guide) is built
         from `.cxd`/`.cx` sources under `docs-src/` by a Make-driven
         pipeline at `scripts/gen_docs/`. The pipeline is itself
         dogfood — the inputs are CX, the templates are CX, the
         outputs are HTML rendered by running the generator with `cx`."""]

    [child id=docgen-make n=8.10.1 title='Make targets'
      [body
        """The top-level Makefile splices `-include
           scripts/gen_docs/docs.mk` to bring the doc targets into
           scope:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'target'] [th 'purpose']]
        [tr [td 'make docs'] [td 'build the site into _docs_staging/']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-diff'] [td 'diff staging vs the live docs/ tree']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-publish'] [td 'copy staging → docs/ with backup on divergence']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-clean'] [td 'wipe _docs_staging/']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-check'] [td 'broken-link + missing-anchor verification']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-all'] [td 'docs + docs-check']]]]

    [child id=docgen-staging n=8.10.2 title='Staging vs live'
      [body
        """`make docs` writes to `_docs_staging/` (in `.gitignore`).
           The live tree at `docs/` is touched only by `make
           docs-publish`, which copies staging → live with a backup
           on any divergence. This separation means doc builds never
           dirty the working tree on incidental builds — only an
           explicit publish step lands changes."""]]

    [child id=docgen-canonical-guide n=8.10.3 title='The canonical guide'
      [body
        """This document — the canonical CX guide — is built by
           `make docs-canonical-guide` from sources under
           `docs-src/canonical/sections/*.cxd`. The pipeline:"""]

      [list
        [item """Reads `docs-src/canonical/manifest.cxd` for the
                table of contents."""]
        [item """For each section file, runs `cx` against the
                rendering template, emitting an HTML fragment."""]
        [item """Concatenates fragments, threads the TOC, expands
                `[[anchor]]` cross-links."""]
        [item """Runs the link-verifier — every `[[anchor]]` and
                every relative URL must resolve."""]
        [item """Writes to `_docs_staging/canonical-guide.html`."""]]

      [note
        """Broken links fail the build. `make docs-check` runs the
           verifier without the render — useful for fast iteration."""]]

    [child id=docgen-snippets n=8.10.4 title='Example snippets'
      [body
        """Code examples in the doc sources can pull from
           `conformance/*.txt` fixtures so they can never drift from
           working code. The `{{EXAMPLE:fixture-id field}}` placeholder
           expands at doc-gen time:"""]

      [example lang=cx
        """[example lang=cx
             '''{{EXAMPLE:cxpath-basic program}}''']
           [output
             '''{{EXAMPLE:cxpath-basic expected}}''']"""]

      [body
        """Outputs are re-recorded from the live `cx` binary, so
           the docs are always pinned to current behaviour. See
           `scripts/gen_docs/helpers/example_expand.cx` for the
           expander logic."""]]]

  [child id=playground n=8.11 title='Playground'
    [intro
      """The CX playground is a browser-embeddable interactive
         evaluator. It runs the same `libcx` core as the CLI,
         compiled to WebAssembly.
         No server round-trip — everything runs in the browser."""]

    [child id=playground-wasm n=8.11.1 title='WASM distribution'
      [body
        """`libcx.wasm` is the WASM build of the V core, exporting
           a **subset** of the C ABI. The exported surface is
           sufficient for: `cx_parse`, `cx_canon`, `cx_hash`,
           `cx_eval`, `cx_code_eval`, `cx_code_diagram`,
           `cx_select`, `cx_lint`, `cx_diff`. The bridge layer
           lives at `tooling/playground/cxlib.js`."""]

      [body
        """ABI capability bits the wasm advertises (per
           [`spec/abi.md`](../../spec/abi.md) §3):"""]

      [list
        [item """**Bit 29** — `_cx_code_diagram` (Mermaid
                containment diagram render)."""]
        [item """**Bit 30** — `_cx_code_tree` (interactive tree
                model for the output pane)."""]
        [item """All earlier cap bits the runtime would otherwise
                advertise on native (0-28)."""]]]

    [child id=playground-component n=8.11.2 title='The <cx-diagram> web component'
      [body
        """`tooling/playground/cx-diagram.js` is a self-contained
           web component that renders a CX document as a Mermaid
           containment diagram. Drop it into any page that loads
           `libcx.wasm`:"""]

      [example lang=html
        """<script type=\"module\" src=\"/cxlib.js\"></script>
           <script type=\"module\" src=\"/cx-diagram.js\"></script>

           <cx-diagram src=\"./users.cx\" max-depth=\"3\"></cx-diagram>"""]

      [body
        """The component reads `src` (URL or inline `<textarea>`
           content), calls `cxlib.code_diagram()` to produce
           Mermaid source, and hands the result to a bundled
           Mermaid renderer. Bidirectional selection: clicking a
           tree node in the output pane highlights the source
           span, and vice versa."""]]

    [child id=playground-tier n=8.11.3 title='Render tiers'
      [body
        """Three tiers per the gate-17 design:"""]

      [list
        [item """**Tier 1** — browser-only Mermaid render. What the
                playground uses. No external service. Currently
                shipped."""]
        [item """**Tier 2** — HTTP service that shells out to
                graphviz for richer (DOT-based) renders. Editor /
                CI integration story. Shipped as
                `tooling/diagram-svc/`."""]
        [item """**Tier 3** — wasm-graphviz inline. Planned
                follow-up. Eliminates the Tier-2 service hop while
                keeping the richer-render output quality."""]]

      [note
        """JSON and XML are **not** valid program sources for the
           diagram — `_cx_code_diagram` accepts CX text only.
           Containment-only relationships, no FK inference. See
           [`spec/audits/playground_gate17_design_v1.md`](../../spec/audits/playground_gate17_design_v1.md)
           for the design rationale."""]]

    [child id=playground-embed n=8.11.4 title='Embedding in a docs site'
      [body
        """The minimum a host page needs:"""]

      [list
        [item """`libcx.wasm` (~ 2.4 MB compressed) served with the
                right MIME type."""]
        [item """`cxlib.js` — the host-side bridge (~ 60 KB)."""]
        [item """Optionally `cx-diagram.js`, `cx-tree.js`, and
                `cx-editor.js` web components."""]]

      [body
        """A full playground page (eval + diagram + tree) ships as
           `tooling/playground/demo.html`. The docs site
           re-uses these same components, so any concept page that
           wants an inline interactive surface gets it for free
           with a one-tag drop-in."""]]]

  [child id=profiling n=8.12 title='Profiling and benchmarking'
    [intro
      """CX ships a standard bench harness for the V core
         (`vcx/tests/runners/code_*_bench.v`) and per-binding bench
         scripts that exercise the FFI path. The bench numbers feed
         into the regression-tracking dashboard and the
         [`spec/governance.md §6`](../../spec/governance.md) SLA
         table."""]

    [child id=profile-eval n=8.12.1 title='Per-directive profiling'
      [body
        """A pprof-compatible per-directive profiler is
           **planned**; no profiling flag ships yet. Today the
           closest tools are the structured error positions, the
           `cx code-tree` view, and the V-native bench harness
           below — see [[diag-trace]]."""]]

    [child id=profile-bench n=8.12.2 title='Bench harness'
      [body
        """The bench harness is V-native; runners live at
           `vcx/tests/runners/code_*_bench.v`. Run individually:"""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ make bench-code-streaming   # streaming evaluator
           $ make bench-code-pattern-compile   # pattern compile cost
           $ make bench-code-http        # http client perf
           $ make bench-code-soak        # long-running soak
           $ make bench-code-cancel      # cancellation responsiveness
           $ make bench-code-gates       # all gate-tracked benches

           $ make bench-streaming        # data-streaming bench
           $ make bench-eval             # general eval cost
           $ make bench-json             # JSON-shape workload"""]

      [body
        """Each bench prints a one-line summary
           (`MB/s`, ops/sec, p50/p95/p99 latency depending on
           dimension) plus a JSON sidecar that the regression
           dashboard consumes."""]]

    [child id=profile-per-binding n=8.12.3 title='Per-binding bench'
      [body
        """The per-binding bench shim is `bench_report.py`. It
           drives every binding through a common harness and
           prints comparative numbers."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ python3 bench_report.py --bindings python,go,rust
           python  parse 1MB: 92ms   eval count(//x): 12ms
           go      parse 1MB: 38ms   eval count(//x): 4ms
           rust    parse 1MB: 31ms   eval count(//x): 3ms"""]

      [note
        """The Go shim has a known SIGSEGV at 1 MB+ inputs as of
           2026-05. The fix is tracked. Python's decoder ceiling
           (~90 ms at 1 MB, pure-Python floor) is architectural —
           a C extension is a planned future candidate."""]]

    [child id=profile-targets n=8.12.4 title='Regression tracking'
      [body
        """The bench numbers are tracked against
           [`spec/governance.md §6`](../../spec/governance.md)
           SLA targets. Current headline numbers:"""]

      [list
        [item """**Streaming evaluator** — 340 MB/s on the
                JSON-shape workload (`bench_streaming_json.v`);
                300 MB/s target met."""]
        [item """**`cx diff` 1 MiB / 1% change** — < 50 ms."""]
        [item """**`cx lint` 1 MiB / all checks** — < 50 ms."""]
        [item """**`cx hash` 1 MiB** — < 30 ms (canonical-bytes +
                SHA-256)."""]]

      [body
        """CI gates on these numbers via a percentile-aware
           regression check: a single run > 1.5× the rolling
           median is flagged; > 2× fails the build."""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/tooling/* and content/reference/cli.cx
     during Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). Compact
     quick-references for the CLI, editor install scripts, the
     tree-sitter grammar, and the conformance/fuzz/bench harnesses. #]

  [child id=tooling-quickref n=8.13 title='Tooling quick references'
    [intro
      """Compact reference snippets for the CLI subcommand surface,
         the editor installer scripts, the tree-sitter grammar
         workflow, and the conformance / fuzz / bench harnesses.
         The primary children above (§8.1-§8.12) carry the
         normative depth; this section is the at-a-glance
         summary."""]

    [child id=cli-quickref n=8.13.1 title='CLI quick reference'
      [body
        """Every subcommand reads from a file or `-` (stdin) and
           writes to stdout unless `--output FILE` is passed.
           Format is auto-detected from the extension or set
           explicitly with `--from FMT` / `--to FMT`."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """# Format conversion (one-shot).
           cx --cx pizza.cx                 # canonical CX form
           cx --xml pizza.cx                # XML
           cx --json pizza.cx               # semantic JSON
           cx --ast pizza.cx                # AST as JSON
           cx --toml pizza.cx               # TOML

           # From-To.
           cx --from=md --to=cx README.md
           cx --from=cx --to=json --compact order.cx
           cx --from=cx --to=xml --lossless order.cx

           # Subcommands.
           cx fmt FILE              # lossless canonical formatter
           cx canonical FILE        # strict canonical text
           cx hash FILE             # SHA-256 over canonical bytes
           cx eq A.cx B.cx          # exit 0 if data-equivalent
           cx diff A.cx B.cx        # semantic diff
           cx lint FILE             # style + correctness
           cx validate FILE --schema=S.cxs
           cx table info FILE
           cx scaffold KIND         # drop a skeleton on stdout
           cx select '//user' FILE  # CXPath query (exit 1 = no match)
           cx version               # build info (same as -v)

           # Run a program (data + program in one input).
           cat data.cx program.cx | cx
           cx pipeline.cx --allow-read --allow-write

           # Run a program against a separate input document ($doc).
           cx program.cx --data=input.cx

           # Include resolution.
           cx --xml --include-root=./conf main.cx     # resolve [?cx include=…]"""]
      [note
        """**Exit codes.** `0` = success (no diagnostics at or
           above the configured `--fail-on` threshold); `1` =
           diagnostics found (lint / validate) or comparison
           returned not-equal; `2` = I/O failure, schema load
           failure, or unrecoverable parse error."""]]

    [child id=editor-installers n=8.13.2 title='Editor installer scripts'
      [body
        """The `tooling/install/` directory ships one shell script
           per editor. Each script writes the editor config,
           registers the `cx lsp` server, and drops the
           syntax-highlighting plugin. The CX language server is
           built into the `cx` binary itself — no separate
           install."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """./tooling/install/install.sh           # interactive — detects editors on PATH
           ./tooling/install/install-vscode.sh    # VS Code only
           ./tooling/install/install-nvim.sh      # Neovim only
           ./tooling/install/install-lsp.sh       # LSP wiring only, no extension
           ./tooling/install/uninstall.sh         # remove everything"""]
      [note
        """**VS Code** — extension at `tooling/vscode`;
           tree-sitter syntax + vscode-languageclient LSP harness;
           supports diagnostics, hover, format-on-save via `cx
           fmt`. **Neovim** — config under `tooling/neovim`;
           install script writes LSP client config and
           tree-sitter grammar. **Other editors** — Helix, Emacs
           (lsp-mode), Sublime Text (LSP plugin), IntelliJ
           (LSP4IJ) drive `cx lsp` directly. Point the editor at
           the `cx` binary; register `.cx` / `.cxd` / `.cxs` as
           file types."""]]

    [child id=tree-sitter-workflow n=8.13.3 title='Tree-sitter grammar workflow'
      [body
        """The tree-sitter grammar lives at
           `tooling/tree-sitter-cx`. It is the source of syntax
           highlighting, code folding, structural selection, and
           lightweight static analysis in every editor. Prebuilt
           artifacts ship inside each editor extension; build
           from source for grammar work."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cd tooling/tree-sitter-cx
           tree-sitter generate
           tree-sitter parse example.cx"""]
      [note
        """Highlight, indent, fold, and locals queries ship
           alongside the grammar. They drive syntax colours,
           structural selection, and cursor-jump-to-definition
           behaviour. Queries are versioned with the grammar;
           updates flow through editor extensions on each
           release."""]]

    [child id=conformance-fuzz-bench n=8.13.4 title='Conformance, fuzzing, benchmarks'
      [body
        """The conformance suite at `conformance/*.txt` is the
           cross-binding contract. Each fixture declares an input
           and an expected output (`out_cx`, `out_xml`, `out_json`,
           `out_text`, or `out_err`). Every binding implements a
           runner that walks the fixture set; a release is gated
           on byte-identical output across every binding."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """# Conformance.
           make test            # all bindings, all suites
           make test-vcx        # V core conformance
           make test-rust       # Rust binding
           make test-python     # Python binding

           # Fuzzers — vcx/tests/fuzz/, structured-random input.
           make fuzz            # 60-second run of every fuzzer
           make fuzz-parser     # parser only, runs until interrupted
           make fuzz-replay     # replay the saved crash corpus

           # Benchmarks — vcx/tests/runners/streaming_bench.v + eval_features_bench.v.
           make bench           # full bench harness, target/bench.json
           scripts/run_bench_json.py --include-eval"""]
      [note
        """Streaming throughput target: 340 MB/s on the comparable
           bench corpus (M2 Pro, libcx `-prod`) — about 17% under
           the comparable JSON benchmark on the same workload."""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/{cookbook,showcase,playground}.cx
     during Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). New
     §8.7 cookbook recipes (13 from content/cookbook + 3 from
     content/showcase) and a playground deep-dive (§8.11 fold). #]

  [child id=cookbook-extras n=8.14 title='Cookbook — additional recipes'
    [intro
      """Recipes carried forward from the pre-Phase-B docs
         cookbook + showcase. The §8.7 children above (recipes
         §8.7.1-§8.7.9) cover the most common nine; this section
         adds 13 more focused recipes."""]

    [child id=recipe-extract n=8.14.1 title='Extract a single value'
      [example lang=shell """echo '//server/@host' | cat config.cx - | cx"""]
      [body
        """Appending a query to its data and running the result
           through `cx` evaluates the query against the preceding
           document. The CXPath finds the `host` attribute of any
           `server` element at any depth."""]]

    [child id=recipe-filter n=8.14.2 title='Filter records by attribute'
      [body
        """A one-line program filters by predicate — the CXPath
           predicate `//user[= $_@role 'admin']` selects only
           admins:"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """echo \"[?for [in \$u //user[= \$_@role 'admin']] [yield \$u]]\" \\
             | cat users.cx - | cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-count n=8.14.3 title='Count records'
      [example lang=shell """echo '[$count //order]' | cat orders.cx - | cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-generate-config n=8.14.4 title='Generate a config from a template'
      [example lang=cx
        """[env host='db.example.com' port=5432 env='prod']
           [?let [= $env [$first //env]]
             [server host=$env@host port=$env@port
                     workers=[?if [= $env@env 'prod'] [then 32] [else 4]]
                     cache=[?if [= $env@env 'prod'] [then '/var/cache/app']
                                                    [else '/tmp/cache']]]]"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cat env.prod.cx server.tpl.cx | cx | tail -1 > server.cx
           cx --toml server.cx > server.toml"""]]

    [child id=recipe-write-stream n=8.14.5 title='Write a stream'
      [example lang=python
        """with cx.stream_writer(open("out.cx", "wb")) as w:
               for record in fetch_records():
                   w.write(record)"""]]

    [child id=recipe-compose-include n=8.14.6 title='Compose a document from files'
      [; check=none — [?cx include] is approved surface (code.md §13); the
         current binary rejects every [?cx …] directive, tracked as engine
         regression #421. Re-enable the check when #421 lands. ]
      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[server
             [?cx include=lib/defaults.cx]
             port=8080
             log-level=info]"""]
      [example lang=shell """cx --cx --include-root=./conf server.cx > resolved.cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-render-template n=8.14.7 title='Render a template with includes'
      [; check=none — [?cx include] is approved surface (code.md §13); the
         current binary rejects every [?cx …] directive (#421). ]
      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[page
             [?cx include=partials/header.cx]
             [?for [in $item //order/line] [yield [li $item@sku]]]
             [?cx include=partials/footer.cx]]"""]
      [example lang=shell """cx --cx --include-root=./templates page.cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-read-parquet n=8.14.8 title='Read a Parquet file into CX'
      [example lang=shell """cx table load orders.parquet --to=cx > orders.cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-anchor-merge n=8.14.9 title='Share defaults across records'
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3]
           [server *shared name=api]
           [server *shared name=worker retries=5]"""]
      [body
        """`&` declares an anchor; `*` merges from it. Local
           fields override; missing ones inherit."""]]

    [child id=recipe-html-safe n=8.14.10 title='HTML-safe templating'
      [; check=none — [?cx output-target] is approved evaluator-config
         surface (grammar.ebnf CXDirective attrs); the current binary
         rejects every [?cx …] directive (#421). ]
      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[?cx output-target=html]
           [h1 //page/@title]
           [?for [in $u //user] [yield [li $u/@name]]]"""]
      [body
        """`[?cx output-target=html]` switches the evaluator into
           context-aware escaping. Substituted values containing
           `<`, `>`, `&`, or quotes escape correctly with no
           manual sprintf."""]]

    [child id=recipe-log-structured n=8.14.11 title='Emit structured log records'
      [example lang=cx
        """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/log']
           [$log:info 'sale' fields={pizza: 'Margherita', price: 12}]
           [$log:warn 'low-stock' fields={pizza: 'Diavola', stock: 2}]"""]
      [body
        """The `log` module emits structured records to stderr
           (default) or a configured sink. Fields are a map;
           level is one of `trace` / `debug` / `info` / `warn` /
           `error`."""]]

    [child id=recipe-logfmt-parser n=8.14.12 title='Logfmt parser in CX code'
      [body
        """Take a logfmt-style line of key=value records and
           split it into structured CX."""]
      [example lang=text """ts=2026-05-20T18:42:00Z level=info request_id=abc123 msg=ok"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings']
           [?let [= $input 'ts=2026-05-20T18:42:00Z level=info msg=ok']
             [event
               [?for [in $kv [$strings:split $input ' ']] [yield
                 [?let [= $pair [$strings:split $kv '=']]
                   [pair name=[$nth $pair 1] value=[$nth $pair 2]]]]]]]"""]]

    [child id=recipe-build-system n=8.14.13 title='Build system in CX'
      [body
        """Targets, depends, run — declared in CX, evaluated by
           a thirty-line CX code driver."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[targets
             [target name=all  depends='lib bin']
             [target name=lib  depends='src/foo.o src/bar.o'  run='ar rcs lib.a src/foo.o src/bar.o']
             [target name=bin  depends='main.o lib'           run='cc -o bin main.o lib.a']]"""]
      [; check=none — illustrative driver: recursion over the target
         graph plus shell execution via cx-stdlib/process (requires
         --allow-subprocess and the target commands on PATH). ]
      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings']
           [?lib 'cx-stdlib/process']
           [?def build impure ($name)
             [?for [in $t //target[= $_@name $name]] [yield
               ([?for [in $d [$strings:split [$string $t@depends] ' ']]
                  [yield [$build $d]]],
                [?if $t@run
                  [then [$process:run ('sh', '-c', [$string $t@run])]]
                  [else ()]])]]]
           [$build 'all']"""]]]

  [child id=playground-detail n=8.15 title='Playground — visualize, offline, highlight'
    [intro
      """Deep-dive on the in-browser playground UI. Section §8.11 above frames the WASM / web
         component / tier story; this section is the operator
         reference for the Tree / Graph / Source-diagram bridge."""]

    [child id=pg-how n=8.15.1 title='How it works'
      [body
        """The playground runs entirely in the browser. Pick a
           starter example from the dropdown, edit it in the
           Source pane, and the three output tabs underneath
           show the canonical CX form, the JSON projection, and
           the XML projection of the parsed tree. No server, no
           signup. Twelve starter examples ship in the corpus
           — three data shapes (atom, nested element, whole-shop)
           and nine program snippets covering the
           directive surface."""]]

    [child id=pg-visualize n=8.15.2 title='Visualize'
      [body
        """The Source pane carries a Diagram toggle in its
           header; the Output pane carries a Tree | Graph view
           toggle adjacent to the CX | JSON | XML projection
           tabs. Tree is the default for every
           fresh source — Graph is opt-in."""]
      [body
        """The Source-pane diagram renders the program as a
           Mermaid graph in a sibling pane. The renderer runs
           entirely in the browser via libcx.wasm — Mermaid text
           emitted by `cx_code_diagram`, then rendered to inline
           SVG by Mermaid.js. Zoom, fit-to-pane, and pan controls
           layer on top."""]
      [body
        """The Output-pane Tree view renders the parsed value as
           an interactive containment-only tree — folder icons
           for elements with body, file-text icons for prose
           leaves, tag and at-sign rows for elements and
           attributes. Built from `cx_code_tree(source)` whose
           JSON contract carries a `{kind, name?,
           value?, loc:{start,end}, children?}` record per node.
           Search, count badges, and standard tree keyboard
           navigation come along for free."""]
      [body
        """The Output-pane Graph view auto-detects mode: sources whose top-level program contains at
           least one `[?directive]` render as a Control-Flow
           Graph (basic blocks; diamonds for `[?if]`; dispatcher
           round-rects with per-arm edges for `[?match]`;
           loop-boxes for `[?for]` with `:bind $x` labels; one
           update-block per `[?modify]`); pure-data sources
           render as an Entity-Relationship Diagram with
           per-instance cardinality inference (`||--o{` for
           repeating children, `||--||` for singletons). Every
           `[?def]` body is its own Mermaid sub-graph."""]
      [note
        """**Bidirectional selection bridge**.
           Click a tree node and the source-pane editor selects
           bytes `[loc.start, loc.end]` and scrolls them into
           view. Move the caret in the source pane and the tree
           pane binary-searches its loc index for the deepest
           containing node and scrolls/selects it. Visualizations
           re-render on Run only — no live debounce — to keep
           the playground predictable."""]]

    [child id=pg-offline n=8.15.3 title='Offline use'
      [body
        """The browser playground is a static page. Clone the
           repo, render the guide with `make guide`, and serve
           `docs/guide/` from any HTTP server (or open via
           `file://`) — the playground works without an internet
           connection. The example corpus, the TypeScript
           bundle, and the CSS all live under
           `scripts/gen_guide/playground/`."""]]

    [child id=pg-highlight n=8.15.4 title='Syntax highlighting'
      [body
        """Every code block on the guide is syntax-highlighted by
           a small in-house JS module at
           `scripts/gen_guide/highlight/`. It speaks the
           languages the docs actually use — `cx`, `shell`,
           `python`, `go`, `rust`, `javascript`, `typescript`,
           `java`, `c#`, `ruby`, `kotlin`, `swift`, `v`, `sql`,
           `ebnf`, `xml`, `html`, `json`, `yaml`, `toml`, `md`.
           The highlighter is regex-based, around 150 lines, no
           external library. The Playground panes use the same
           module on every Run."""]]]]
